---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { SITE } from '../data/site';
import { CLOCKS } from '../data/clocks';
import { LICENSES } from '../data/licenses';
import { STATES } from '../data/states';

const pages = STATES.length * LICENSES.length;
---

<Layout
  title="About — FastLicense"
  description="FastLicense is Hanok’s cited timeline desk for Cornerstone’s four U.S. licensing cores. A number is published only when a statute or a regulator page is."
>
  <section class="masthead">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">About</p>
      <h1>A launch date should survive a public source.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        FastLicense is the timeline desk for collection, consumer lending, money transmission, and
        mortgage company licenses in the United States. Hanok keeps the citations. Cornerstone Licensing
        prepares and files the work.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>The question that kept coming back</h2>
      <p>
        Someone on the team always asks how long the license will take. The useful answer depends on
        the state, the core, and whether the agency has published a number at all. This directory exists
        so that answer is a page you can open — a statute, a regulator notice, or a deliberate blank —
        before it hardens into a date on a slide.
      </p>

      <h2>Who it is for</h2>
      <p>
        Lenders, mortgage companies, money services businesses, and accounts receivable firms building
        a U.S. filing calendar. Teams start here from the United States and from abroad, including
        readers who arrive at fastlicence.com. The index is national: the same four cores, the same
        editorial rule, in every state.
      </p>

      <h2>Who keeps the pages</h2>
      <p>
        FastLicense is a Hanok satellite, kept for Cornerstone’s operators. Jeff Brewer owns the desk.
        The editorial rule is narrow on purpose: if a figure is not on a public statute or a regulator
        page, the typical range stays blank. There is no gallery of stock portraits. The citation is the
        credential. Sources on this build were accessed {SITE.accessed}. {CLOCKS.length} clocks earned a
        number. The other pages among the {pages} pairs did not.
      </p>
      <p>
        Filings, fees, and issued licenses stay with
        <a href={SITE.cornerstone.url}>{SITE.cornerstone.name}</a>, the operating partner for those four
        cores. A timeline review of a real file starts at
        <a href={SITE.cornerstone.contact}>Ask Cornerstone</a>, or by phone at {SITE.cornerstone.phone}.
      </p>

      <h2>Three desks, one filing partner</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>This site</strong> answers how long a filed application can sit, what commonly stops the
        clock, and when an expedite is actually published.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href={SITE.sisters.simple.url}>{SITE.sisters.simple.name}</a> answers {SITE.sisters.simple.job}.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href={SITE.sisters.store.url}>{SITE.sisters.store.name}</a> answers {SITE.sisters.store.job}.
      </p>
      <p>
        An FCA authorisation timetable is a different desk. It is not part of this directory. If the
        filing is in a U.S. state, the clock to read is the one on that state’s page.
      </p>

      <p style="margin-top:28px">
        <a class="btn btn-solid" href={SITE.cornerstone.contact} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ask Cornerstone</a>
      </p>
      <p>
        The editorial rule, in full, is on <a href="/methodology/">methods</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
